name bvr-duel-decided-1
timeout 300

# READING-RULE UPDATE (model delta D1, flaperon fix): this mission currently ends TIMEOUT, exit 3.
# The launch geometry rides on the corrected roll behaviour; the decisive shot now misses by 7.09 m —
# still inside the 10 m fuze, but the 1/r^2 damage no longer kills. A re-tune round against the
# corrected model is queued (doc/flightbox/journal.md).

# THE DECIDED DUEL: the same two intercept pilots as bvr-duel.fbm, the same code on both sides, the same
# loadout, the same countermeasure programme, the same objectives — and a different STARTING GEOMETRY.
# bvr-duel is a near-mirror (1,000 m and 50 kt of asymmetry) and ends in a stalemate: both shoot, both
# rounds run out of fuel, both jets land the run alive at the timeout. That draw is a PROPERTY OF THE
# SYMMETRY, not a property of the pilot, which is exactly why it could not decide anything.
#
# WHAT IS ASYMMETRIC HERE, and why it is a geometry question rather than a code one:
#   viper   12,000 m / 500 kt      bandit   6,000 m / 350 kt
# 6,000 m of altitude and 150 kt of speed. A BVR launch zone is an INTEGRATION that starts from the
# launcher's own velocity and the air it is flying in (modules/f16/FBF16FireControl::SolveLaunchZone),
# so the higher, faster jet's Rtr — the range from which its round arrives even if the target turns and
# runs — is far larger than the lower, slower one's. Both pilots use the identical rule ("designate at
# the briefed lock range, shoot at Rtr"), both designate in the SAME TENTH OF A SECOND (t=96.6, they are
# co-ranged), and then the energy decides: viper's Rtr arrives first and its trigger goes at t=101.3
# while the bandit is still closing to its own. From that moment the bandit's receiver is answering a
# LAUNCH rather than working an attack, and it never gets a shot away at all.
#
# THE TIMELINE THE RUN PRODUCES (measured):
#   t=96.6   both designate — RADAR_DESIGNATE on both sides, same second, same range (14.9 nm)
#   t=101.3  viper's trigger, LAUNCH_SOLUTION rangeM=24661 rtrM=24947 — inside Rtr by 300 m
#   t=101.4  the bandit's RWR goes from `track` to `missile`: it is now defending, not attacking
#   t=124.3  DETONATION 1.58 m — every system of the bandit fails, it is combat-ineffective
#   t=124.4  the bandit's own mission monitor: FAIL, "combat ineffective (survive objective lost)"
#   t=163.4  the wreck reaches the ground. That K.O. was somebody's DECLARED objective, so it ends the
#            run without deciding it (doc/mission-format.md, "Urteil"): the viper is finalised instead
#            and concludes SUCCESS, "objectives met, survived". Exit 0.
#
# BOTH SIDES DECLARE THE SAME TWO OBJECTIVES, which is what makes this readable as a duel rather than as
# one jet's sortie: kill him, and come home. The verdict is per unit and the two verdicts are opposite —
# a winner and a loser, not two failures.

unit viper
  team friendly
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 6.40000 12000 90.0 500
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set task intercept
  wp 46.90000 8.35000 12000 500            # the vector: threat bearing 090
  objective kill unit bandit
  objective survive

unit bandit
  team hostile
  module f16
  spawn 46.90000 7.55000 6000 270.0 350
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode crm
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set cmds_mode man
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set task intercept
  wp 46.90000 5.75000 6000 350             # its mirror vector, threat bearing 270
  objective kill unit viper
  objective survive
